    <string name="academy_module_1_title">The Club Primer</string>
    <string name="academy_module_1_subtitle">The Fundamental Laws</string>
    <string name="academy_module_1_desc">Formal rules, mandatory capture with single card vs sum, and value of the Settebello.</string>
    <string name="academy_module_1_rule">A single matching card always takes absolute precedence over any sum on the table.</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step1_title">The Single Card Rule</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step1_speech">A strict rule applies at the club: on the table lie a 7 of Swords, a 4 of Cups, and a 3 of Batons. In hand you hold the 7 of Coins. May you choose to take 4 and 3, leaving the 7?</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step1_badge">Mandatory single capture</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step1_opt1">No, the rules require capturing the single card</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step1_fb1">Correct! Rule 3: the single card always commands over any combination!</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step1_opt2">Yes, I can freely choose what to take</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step1_fb2">Common mistake! If a matching single card is present, capturing it is mandatory by law.</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step2_title">The King of Coins</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step2_speech">The Settebello alone is worth up to 3 deck points out of 4: clean point, gold for Coins, and 21 for Primiera. Don\'t let it slip!</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step2_badge">Capture the Settebello!</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step3_title">Choosing Between Sums</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step3_speech">Now look: on the table are 5 and 2 of Swords, and 4 and 3 of Coins. You hold a 7. There is no single 7 on the board. Which pair should you capture?</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step3_badge">Two valid combinations</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step3_opt1">Take the 4 and 3 of Coins to secure gold points</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step3_fb1">Perfect! When no single card exists, the choice is yours: always bag Coins and useful cards!</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step3_opt2">Take the 5 and 2 of Swords indifferently</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step3_fb2">Careful: leaving Coins on the board gifts easy points to your opponent!</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step4_title">Primer Completed</string>
    <string name="academy_m1_step4_speech">Well done! You now know the primary laws of the club: mandatory single capture, protected Settebello, and strategic focus on Coins. Here is your badge!</string>
    <string name="academy_module_2_title">The Secret of Primiera</string>
    <string name="academy_module_2_subtitle">The Hidden Mathematics</string>
    <string name="academy_module_2_desc">Quick calculation of point values (7=21, 6=18, Ace=16, 5=15) and defending all 4 suits.</string>
    <string name="academy_module_2_rule">Primiera rewards covering all four suits: an empty suit zeroes out even three splendid Sevens.</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step1_title">The Paradox of the King</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step1_speech">Many think face cards are worth a lot. In Primiera the scale is inverted: 7 is 21, 6 is 18, Ace is 16. How much is a King worth in Primiera?</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step1_badge">Scale: 7=21, 6=18, Ace=16, 5=15, Face=10</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step1_opt1">Only 10 points, even less than a Two (12 pt)</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step1_fb1">Exactly! Kings and Jacks are only worth 10 points. The true rulers of Primiera are Sevens and Sixes!</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step1_opt2">20 points, because it is the highest card in the deck</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step1_fb2">No! Don\'t let the crown fool you: all face cards are only worth 10 points in Primiera!</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step2_title">The Empty Suit Trap</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step2_speech">You already captured three Sevens (Swords, Cups, and Coins), but zero Batons! On the table lies the 5 of Batons. Capture it immediately to cover the fourth suit!</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step2_badge">Protect all 4 suits</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step3_title">Counting the Sevens</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step3_speech">Both players have covered all 4 suits. You captured 3 Sevens and a Six (81 pt). Your opponent has 1 Seven and three Sixes (75 pt). Who wins Primiera?</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step3_badge">Calculation: 21+21+21+18 = 81 pt</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step3_opt1">You win: 81 points against your opponent\'s 75 points</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step3_fb1">Flawless mathematics! Whoever has more Sevens with all suits covered dominates the count.</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step3_opt2">It\'s a tie because both have high cards</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step3_fb2">No: Primiera is calculated by summing the exact values of each suit (81 to 75)!</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step4_title">Primiera Mastered</string>
    <string name="academy_m2_step4_speech">Magnificent! Primiera holds no more secrets for you: you know the real card values and the vital importance of never leaving a suit empty.</string>
    <string name="academy_module_3_title">The Odd Rule</string>
    <string name="academy_module_3_subtitle">Risk Control &amp; Defense</string>
    <string name="academy_module_3_desc">The critical threshold of 10, how to avoid gifting Sweeps, and the dealer\'s final hand privilege.</string>
    <string name="academy_module_3_rule">If the sum on the table exceeds 10, no single card can make a Sweep: the table is safe.</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step1_title">The Threshold of Ten</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step1_speech">On the table lies a 4. You cannot capture. In hand you hold a 3 and a King (10). Playing 3 makes the sum 7; playing King makes 14. Which play protects you from a Sweep?</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step1_badge">Sum > 10 = Zero Sweep risk</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step1_opt1">The King: raising the sum to 14 makes a single-card Sweep impossible</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step1_fb1">Hats off! No single card equals 14: when the sum exceeds 10, the board is completely safe!</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step1_opt2">The 3: it\'s a lower card and less appealing to the opponent</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step1_fb2">Danger! With 4+3=7 any opponent holding a 7 lands an immediate Sweep and cleans the board!</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step2_title">Fortifying the Board</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step2_speech">On the board lies a 6 of Batons. You have no valid captures. Play the Horse (9) to push the total to 15 and prevent gifting a Sweep!</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step2_badge">Exceed 10</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step3_title">The Dealer\'s Final Hand</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step3_speech">We are at the very last play of the deal. Either you or your opponent captures all cards on the table. Does it count as a Sweep?</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step3_badge">Final capture of the deal</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step3_opt1">No, by law the last capture of the deal never counts as a Sweep</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step3_fb1">Fundamental club rule! On the final trick, Sweeps are never awarded: remaining cards go to whoever made the last capture.</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step3_opt2">Yes, emptying the table is always a Sweep</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step3_fb2">False! The final rule expressly forbids awarding a Sweep on the last play.</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step4_title">Master of the Board</string>
    <string name="academy_m3_step4_speech">Heartfelt congratulations! You completed the first 3 fundamental modules of the Academy. You now think with the mathematical lucidity and discipline of true champions!</string>
</resources>
